WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:16.250
When we're young, we're innocently brave,

00:00:16.292 --> 00:00:19.809
and we fearlessly dream
about what our lives might be like.

00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.351
Maybe you wanted to be an astronaut
or a rocket scientist.

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:27.143
Maybe you dreamed
of traveling to every continent.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:28.851
Since I was very young,

00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:31.059
I dreamed of working
for the United Nations

00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:34.059
in some of the most difficult
countries in the world.

00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:36.393
And thanks to a lot of courage

00:00:36.417 --> 00:00:38.518
that dream came true.

00:00:38.542 --> 00:00:40.559
But here's the thing about courage:

00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:43.184
it doesn't just appear
whenever we need it.

00:00:43.208 --> 00:00:46.518
It's the result of tough
reflection and real work,

00:00:46.542 --> 00:00:51.018
involving the balance
between fear and bravery.

00:00:51.042 --> 00:00:53.851
Without fear, we'll do foolish things.

00:00:53.875 --> 00:00:57.893
And without courage,
we'll never step into the unknown.

00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:00.768
The balance of the two
is where the magic lies,

00:01:00.792 --> 00:01:03.833
and it's a balance
we all deal with every day.

00:01:04.958 --> 00:01:07.393
First, a word about my fancy wheels.

00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:09.476
I haven't always used a wheelchair.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.976
I grew up like many of you,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.184
running, jumping and dancing.

00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:16.059
I love to dance.

00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:18.351
However, in my mid-twenties,

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:22.476
I began to experience
a series of inexplicable falls.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.809
And a few years later,

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:27.184
I was diagnosed with a recessive
genetic condition

00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:30.851
called hereditary inclusion body myopathy,

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:33.393
or HIBM.

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:36.226
It's a progressive muscle wasting disease

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.893
that affects all of my muscles
from head to toe.

00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:42.059
HIBM is very rare.

00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:47.476
In the United States there are
less than 200 people diagnosed.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:51.684
To date, there is
no proved treatment or cure,

00:01:51.708 --> 00:01:54.768
and within 10 to 15 years of its onset,

00:01:54.792 --> 00:01:57.684
HIBM typically leads to quadriplegia,

00:01:57.708 --> 00:02:00.643
which is why I now use a wheelchair.

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:04.268
When I was first diagnosed,
everything changed.

00:02:04.292 --> 00:02:06.268
It was frightening news

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:10.643
because I had no experience
with chronic illness or disabilities.

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:15.059
And I had no idea
how the disease might progress.

00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:17.184
But what was most disheartening

00:02:17.208 --> 00:02:20.351
was to listen to other people advise me

00:02:20.375 --> 00:02:22.518
to limit my ambitions and dreams,

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:26.208
and to change my expectations
of what to expect from life.

00:02:27.208 --> 00:02:30.018
"You should quit
your international career."

00:02:30.042 --> 00:02:32.684
"No one will marry you this way."

00:02:32.708 --> 00:02:35.417
"You would be selfish to have children."

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.976
The fact that someone who wasn't me

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.851
was putting limitations
on my dreams and ambitions

00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:43.643
was preposterous.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.292
And unacceptable.

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:48.143
So I ignored them.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:54.684
(Cheers and applause)

00:02:54.708 --> 00:02:56.351
I did get married.

00:02:56.375 --> 00:03:00.184
And I decided for myself
not to have children.

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:03.518
And I continued my career
with the United Nations

00:03:03.542 --> 00:03:04.809
after my diagnosis,

00:03:04.833 --> 00:03:07.268
going to work for two years in Angola,

00:03:07.292 --> 00:03:11.208
a country recovering
from 27 years of brutal civil war.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:14.059
However, it would be another five years

00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:17.893
until I officially declared
my diagnosis to my employer.

00:03:17.917 --> 00:03:19.226
Because I was afraid

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:23.601
that they would question my capacity
to manage and I'd lose my job.

00:03:23.625 --> 00:03:26.851
I was working in countries
where polio had been common,

00:03:26.875 --> 00:03:28.643
so when I overheard someone say

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:31.393
that they thought
I might have survived polio,

00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:34.018
I thought my secret was safe.

00:03:34.042 --> 00:03:36.351
No one asked why I was limping.

00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:38.559
So I didn't say anything.

00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:39.934
It took me over a decade

00:03:39.958 --> 00:03:43.143
to internalize the severity of HIBM,

00:03:43.167 --> 00:03:47.809
even as basic tasks and functions
became increasingly difficult.

00:03:47.833 --> 00:03:51.893
Yet, I continued to pursue my dream
of working all over the world,

00:03:51.917 --> 00:03:54.309
and was even appointed
as a disability focal point

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.226
for UNICEF in Haiti,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.125
where I served for two years
after the devastating 2010 earthquake.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.893
And then my work brought me
to the United States.

00:04:03.917 --> 00:04:06.684
And even as the disease
progressed significantly

00:04:06.708 --> 00:04:09.726
and I needed leg braces
and a walker to get around,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.726
I still longed for adventure.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.018
And this time,

00:04:14.042 --> 00:04:17.434
I started dreaming
of a grand outdoor adventure.

00:04:17.458 --> 00:04:20.917
And what's more grand
than the Grand Canyon?

00:04:21.875 --> 00:04:26.268
Did you know that for every
five million people who visit the Rim

00:04:26.292 --> 00:04:29.542
only one percent go down
to the canyon's base?

00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:33.393
I wanted to be a part of that one percent.

00:04:33.417 --> 00:04:34.934
The only thing is --

00:04:34.958 --> 00:04:39.309
(Applause)

00:04:39.333 --> 00:04:43.726
The only thing is that the Grand Canyon
isn't exactly accessible.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.476
I was going to need some assistance

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.434
to get down the 5,000-foot descent
of vertical loose terrain.

00:04:50.458 --> 00:04:52.018
Now, when I face obstacles,

00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:55.809
fear doesn't necessarily
immediately set in

00:04:55.833 --> 00:04:58.643
because I assume that one way or another,

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:00.226
I'll figure it out.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.476
And in this case, my thought was,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.643
well, if I can't walk down,

00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:06.851
I could learn to ride a horse.

00:05:06.875 --> 00:05:08.809
So that's what I did.

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:10.643
And with that fateful decision

00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.643
began a four-year commitment,

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:15.726
tossing back and forth
between fear and courage

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.476
to undertake a 12-day expedition.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.726
Four days on horseback
to cross Grand Canyon rim to rim,

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.768
and eight days rafting
150 miles of the Colorado River,

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:27.726
all with a film crew in tow.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.059
Spoiler alert -- we made it.

00:05:30.083 --> 00:05:32.893
But not without showing me
how my deepest fear

00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:37.768
can somehow manifest
a mirror response of equal courage.

00:05:37.792 --> 00:05:40.976
On April 13, 2018,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.726
sitting eight feet above the ground,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.976
riding a mustang horse named Sheriff,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.268
my first impression of Grand Canyon

00:05:47.292 --> 00:05:50.184
was one of shock and terror.

00:05:50.208 --> 00:05:53.018
Who knew I had a fear of heights.

00:05:53.042 --> 00:05:54.309
(Laughter)

00:05:54.333 --> 00:05:56.851
But there was no giving up now.

00:05:56.875 --> 00:06:00.601
I mustered up every ounce
of courage inside me

00:06:00.625 --> 00:06:04.101
to not let my fear get the best of me.

00:06:04.125 --> 00:06:05.934
Embarking on the South Rim,

00:06:05.958 --> 00:06:08.684
all I could do to keep myself composed

00:06:08.708 --> 00:06:12.351
was to breathe deeply,
stare up into the clouds

00:06:12.375 --> 00:06:15.893
and focus on my team's voices.

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:20.476
But then, in the first hour,
disaster struck.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.851
Unable to hold myself
upright in the saddle,

00:06:22.875 --> 00:06:24.851
going down an oversized step,

00:06:24.875 --> 00:06:29.393
I flung forward and smacked my face
on the back of the horse's head.

00:06:29.417 --> 00:06:30.684
There was panic,

00:06:30.708 --> 00:06:32.268
my head hurt fiercely,

00:06:32.292 --> 00:06:35.976
but the path was too narrow
for us to dismount.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.809
Only at the halfway point at 2,300 feet,

00:06:38.833 --> 00:06:41.059
at least another two hours down,

00:06:41.083 --> 00:06:43.184
could we stop and remove my helmet

00:06:43.208 --> 00:06:47.143
and see the egg-sized bump
protruding from my forehead.

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:49.226
For all of that planning and gear,

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.059
how is it that we didn't
even have an ice pack?

00:06:52.083 --> 00:06:53.101
(Laughter)

00:06:53.125 --> 00:06:56.018
Luckily for all of us,
the swelling came outwards,

00:06:56.042 --> 00:07:00.059
and would drain into my face
as two fantastic black eyes

00:07:00.083 --> 00:07:03.143
which is an amazing way to look
in a documentary film.

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:05.226
(Laughter)

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:10.309
(Applause and cheers)

00:07:10.333 --> 00:07:12.476
This was not an easy, peaceful journey,

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.559
and yet, that was exactly the point.

00:07:14.583 --> 00:07:17.184
Even though I was afraid
to get back into the saddle,

00:07:17.208 --> 00:07:18.559
I got back in.

00:07:18.583 --> 00:07:20.934
The descent alone to the canyon floor

00:07:20.958 --> 00:07:22.851
took a total of 10 hours

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:25.768
and that was just day one of four riding.

00:07:25.792 --> 00:07:27.851
Next came the mighty rapids.

00:07:27.875 --> 00:07:29.851
The Colorado River in the Grand Canyon

00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:32.768
has some of the highest
white water in the country.

00:07:32.792 --> 00:07:35.643
And just to be prepared
in case we should capsize,

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:38.476
we'd practice having me swim
through a smaller rapid.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.184
And it's safe to say it wasn't glamorous.

00:07:41.208 --> 00:07:42.268
(Laughter)

00:07:42.292 --> 00:07:44.809
I took my breath
in the wrong part of the wave,

00:07:44.833 --> 00:07:46.268
choked on river water

00:07:46.292 --> 00:07:48.809
and was unable to steer myself.

00:07:48.833 --> 00:07:50.393
Yes, it was scary,

00:07:50.417 --> 00:07:52.809
but it was also fantastic.

00:07:52.833 --> 00:07:54.976
Waterfalls, slick canyons

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.934
and a couple billion years of bedrock

00:07:56.958 --> 00:07:59.434
that seemed to change color
throughout the day.

00:07:59.458 --> 00:08:02.351
The Grand Canyon is true wilderness

00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:05.059
and worthy of all of its accolades.

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:08.792
(Applause)

00:08:12.833 --> 00:08:14.226
The expedition,

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:17.184
all that planning and the trip itself,

00:08:17.208 --> 00:08:22.226
showed me a level of fear
I had never experienced before.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.643
But more importantly,

00:08:23.667 --> 00:08:28.059
it showed me how boldly
courageous I can be.

00:08:28.083 --> 00:08:30.351
My Grand Canyon journey was not easy.

00:08:30.375 --> 00:08:33.018
This was not a vision
of an Amazonian woman

00:08:33.042 --> 00:08:36.559
effortlessly making her way
through epic scenery.

00:08:36.583 --> 00:08:38.268
This was me crying,

00:08:38.292 --> 00:08:42.184
exhausted and beat up with two black eyes.

00:08:42.208 --> 00:08:43.601
It was scary,

00:08:43.625 --> 00:08:45.601
it was stressful,

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:47.917
it was exhilarating.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:50.268
Now that the trip is over,

00:08:50.292 --> 00:08:53.809
it's easy to be blasé
about what we achieved.

00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:56.351
I know I want to raft the river again.

00:08:56.375 --> 00:08:59.768
This time, all 277 miles of it.

00:08:59.792 --> 00:09:03.226
(Applause)

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:06.601
But I also know that I would never do
the horseback-riding part again.

00:09:06.625 --> 00:09:08.268
(Laughter)

00:09:08.292 --> 00:09:10.851
It's just too dangerous.

00:09:10.875 --> 00:09:12.518
And that's my real point.

00:09:12.542 --> 00:09:15.434
I'm not just here
to show you my film footage.

00:09:15.458 --> 00:09:17.476
I'm here to remind us all

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.726
that life is really just a lesson

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.809
in finding the balance
between fear and courage.

00:09:22.833 --> 00:09:27.643
And understanding what is
and what isn't a good idea.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:28.893
(Laughter)

00:09:28.917 --> 00:09:30.643
Life is already scary,

00:09:30.667 --> 00:09:34.393
so for our dreams to come true,
we need to be brave.

00:09:34.417 --> 00:09:35.768
In facing my fears

00:09:35.792 --> 00:09:38.393
and finding the courage
to push through them,

00:09:38.417 --> 00:09:42.101
I swear my life has been extraordinary.

00:09:42.125 --> 00:09:43.559
So live big

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:46.518
and try to let your courage
outweigh your fear.

00:09:46.542 --> 00:09:49.476
You never know where it might take you.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.768
Thank you.

00:09:50.792 --> 00:09:54.250
(Applause and cheers)

